{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "missing_requirement",
      "description": "ngrok-watchdog.service is not mentioned anywhere in the plan. It exists at ~/.config/systemd/user/ngrok-watchdog.service and depends on ngrok-tunnel.service, but the plan only addresses ngrok-tunnel.service in Task 6. The watchdog service should also be reviewed for hardening (it currently has no restart limits and references a Python script).",
      "recommendation": "Add ngrok-watchdog.service to Task 6 scope. At minimum, verify the referenced script (scripts/ngrok_watchdog.py) exists and add resource limits. If the script doesn't exist, treat it like cohezion-lab (disable or remove)."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "missing_requirement",
      "description": "cohezion-simulation.service and cohezion-simulation.timer exist as templates in /home/mike-anderson/dev/cohezion/systemd/ but are not addressed in the plan. The plan's Context section mentions them but no task reviews or hardens them. If they are installed or could be installed, they need the same hardening treatment.",
      "recommendation": "Add a check in Task 5 or Task 6 to verify whether cohezion-simulation.service/timer are installed (symlinked). If installed, harden them. If only templates, add restart limits to the templates for future use."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "verification",
      "description": "Task 7 guardian script verification is limited to syntax check and a dry run. There is no concrete test that the guardian actually detects and stops a crash-looping service. The Definition of Done says 'Guardian detects a simulated crash loop (test with a mock)' but the Verify section only has 'bash -n' and a plain run.",
      "recommendation": "Add a concrete verification step: create a temporary dummy service that crash-loops, run the guardian, and confirm it stops the dummy service. This proves the detection logic works end-to-end."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "ambiguity",
      "description": "Task 3 adds an ExecStartPre that validates MANIFEST existence, but the exact implementation is not specified. The MANIFEST filename changes over time (190877, 190909, etc.), so a hardcoded check would break. The check should verify that the file referenced by CURRENT exists.",
      "recommendation": "Specify the ExecStartPre command explicitly, e.g.: ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'test -f /home/mike-anderson/dev/cohezion/data/surrealdb/$(cat /home/mike-anderson/dev/cohezion/data/surrealdb/CURRENT)' to dynamically check the CURRENT pointer."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "scope",
      "description": "The plan correctly notes SURREAL_PASS regeneration as out of scope, but does not mention that every restart of cohezion-surreal.service generates a different password, meaning anything connecting to SurrealDB after a restart may use a stale password. This could cause confusing failures during Task 8 verification.",
      "recommendation": "Add a note in Task 8's Key Decisions that the SURREAL_PASS regeneration issue may affect connectivity tests, and specify how to work around it during verification (e.g., source .env before connecting, or note the generated password)."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "pass",
  "summary": "The plan is thorough, well-structured, and accurately captures the user's requirements. The root cause analysis is detailed and matches the actual system state (verified: CURRENT points to MANIFEST-190877, only MANIFEST-190909 exists, 58K+ files in data dir). All 8 scope items from the user's request are covered. Task dependencies are correct. Verification commands are concrete and testable. Two minor gaps: ngrok-watchdog.service and cohezion-simulation templates are not explicitly addressed but should be reviewed during hardening. The remaining findings are suggestions for implementation clarity. No must_fix issues found."
}

{
  "findings": [
    {
      "severity": "must_fix",
      "category": "assumption",
      "description": "SURREAL_PASS uses $(python3 -c ...) shell substitution in .env, but systemd EnvironmentFile does NOT evaluate shell commands. It reads the literal string. This means SurrealDB is receiving a literal '$(python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(24))\")' as the password, not an actual token. The plan marks this as 'out of scope' (line 83, 352), but if the MANIFEST fix succeeds and SurrealDB starts, it will initialize with a literal garbage password. Then if the service ever restarts, it gets the SAME literal password (so it won't mismatch), but any client connecting with a shell-evaluated password from sourcing .env will fail to authenticate. This is not a crash-loop risk but it IS a broken authentication that will surface immediately in Task 8 verification when trying to connect to verify SurrealDB health.",
      "recommendation": "Either (a) add a note in Task 8 that authentication testing may fail due to the SURREAL_PASS issue and document the workaround, or (b) generate a static password and update .env as part of Task 3. Given that systemd doesn't evaluate $(), the 'regenerates on each restart' concern in the plan (line 83) is actually wrong - it's a static literal string to systemd. But it's still broken for any shell-based client."
    },
    {
      "severity": "must_fix",
      "category": "assumption",
      "description": "The plan assumes fixing the CURRENT pointer to MANIFEST-190909 will repair the database (Task 1). However, MANIFEST-190909 was written at Feb 14 18:40 - the same timestamp as when CURRENT was last modified. The original MANIFEST-190877 that CURRENT pointed to is gone. There's only 1 SST file (190912.sst) and 1 WAL file (190908.log). The MANIFEST-190909 is only 518 bytes. If MANIFEST-190909 references SST files or WAL files that have been lost or were never properly flushed before the corruption event, fixing the pointer alone won't help - RocksDB will fail with a different error (missing SST file or checksum mismatch). The plan's Risk table mentions this (line 348) but rates it 'Low' likelihood. With a 518-byte manifest referencing potentially orphaned data across 58K files of crash debris, likelihood should be Medium.",
      "recommendation": "Add a validation step after fixing CURRENT: attempt to open the database with a read-only RocksDB tool or run 'surreal start' in a temporary test mode. If it fails with a different error after the MANIFEST fix, immediately fall back to reinitializing with a fresh database (as the risk table suggests). Don't wait until Task 8 to discover this."
    },
    {
      "severity": "must_fix",
      "category": "failure_mode",
      "description": "The service is CURRENTLY crash-looping RIGHT NOW (confirmed: NRestarts=2248 since last boot, cohezion-lab also in 'activating (auto-restart)' with status=216/GROUP). The plan's Task 1 says 'Stop the service first: sudo systemctl stop cohezion-surreal.service' but then says 'Do NOT start the service yet - wait for Task 3 to harden it first'. However, between Task 1 (stop + fix CURRENT) and Task 3 (harden service file), Task 2 runs (cleanup). If the implementer runs 'sudo systemctl stop' but doesn't also 'sudo systemctl disable' or mask the service, a system reboot during implementation would restart the crash loop with the old unhardened config. The plan should explicitly mask or disable the service in Task 1, not just stop it.",
      "recommendation": "In Task 1, add: 'sudo systemctl mask cohezion-surreal.service' after stopping it, and 'systemctl --user mask cohezion-lab.service'. Then unmask in Task 3/Task 4 after hardening. This prevents accidental re-enabling during the multi-task implementation window."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "architecture",
      "description": "The guardian script runs every 5 minutes via systemd timer (Task 7). The plan says it checks for '>3 restarts in 10 minutes'. But the current crash loop restarts every 5 seconds (SurrealDB) or 60 seconds (cohezion-lab). At 5-second intervals, SurrealDB would accumulate 60 restarts in 5 minutes before the guardian even runs once. With the proposed RestartSec=30 (Task 3), that's still 10 restarts in 5 minutes. The guardian will always be reactive, not preventive - it can only stop a crash loop AFTER it's been running for up to 5 minutes. The systemd StartLimitBurst is the actual prevention mechanism (stops after 5 restarts in 10 minutes). The guardian is therefore a belt-and-suspenders check, but the plan presents it as a primary defense ('detects crash-looping services and stops them before they cascade'). This is optimistic framing.",
      "recommendation": "Clarify in the plan that StartLimitBurst/StartLimitIntervalSec is the PRIMARY crash-loop prevention mechanism, and the guardian is a SECONDARY safety net that catches cases where: (a) a service doesn't have proper limits configured, (b) a service reaches its burst limit and systemd puts it in 'failed' state but an admin manually restarts it, or (c) the crash loop is slow enough to stay under burst limits but still problematic over hours. Also consider reducing the timer to 2 minutes for faster detection."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "failure_mode",
      "description": "The guardian script (Task 7) monitors 'all cohezion-* services' but cohezion-surreal.service is a SYSTEM service (in /etc/systemd/system/), while the guardian timer runs as a USER service (in ~/.config/systemd/user/). A user-scoped systemd timer can query system services via 'systemctl status cohezion-surreal.service' (read-only is fine), but it CANNOT stop a system service with 'systemctl stop cohezion-surreal.service' - that requires sudo/root. The plan doesn't address this privilege gap. The guardian can detect the SurrealDB crash loop but cannot actually stop it.",
      "recommendation": "Either (a) move cohezion-surreal.service to a user service (runs as mike-anderson anyway, per the User= directive), (b) use a polkit rule to allow the user to stop the specific service without sudo, (c) make the guardian a system-level timer for system services, or (d) have the guardian use 'systemctl --user stop' for user services and 'loginctl terminate-user' / desktop notification for system services it can't stop. Option (a) is cleanest - SurrealDB already runs as mike-anderson, so there's no need for it to be a system service."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "completeness",
      "description": "The cascade chain analysis (lines 37-44) claims 'cohezion-lab also crash-looping (User= bug in user service, 60s interval)' contributed to the desktop crash. I confirmed cohezion-lab IS crash-looping with exit status 216/GROUP (the supplementary groups error). However, exit status 216 means systemd itself rejected the service before any process was spawned - this is extremely lightweight (no fork, no memory allocation, no I/O). It generates journal entries but no process churn. The plan overstates its contribution to the cascade. The real damage came from SurrealDB's 129K+ actual process spawns (each initializing RocksDB) combined with journald bloat.",
      "recommendation": "Adjust the cascade chain to reflect that cohezion-lab contributed journal noise but not process churn. This matters because it affects prioritization: fixing SurrealDB's crash loop is critical, fixing cohezion-lab is important but not urgent for system stability."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "optimism",
      "description": "The plan says MemoryMax=16G will 'prevent the 66GB block cache from consuming all RAM' (line 171). However, RocksDB's block cache is allocated in-process. If SurrealDB tries to allocate 66GB and hits a 16GB cgroup limit, the OOM killer will terminate the process. This will be treated as a crash by systemd, triggering a restart, which will try to allocate 66GB again, triggering another OOM kill - creating a NEW crash loop (albeit rate-limited by StartLimitBurst). The correct fix is to configure RocksDB's block_cache_size in SurrealDB's configuration, not just set a cgroup memory limit. A cgroup limit without application-level configuration turns an unbounded allocation into an OOM crash loop.",
      "recommendation": "Research SurrealDB's RocksDB configuration options (likely via command-line flags or config file) to set block_cache_size explicitly (e.g., 4GB or 8GB). Set MemoryMax to 16G as a hard safety net, but also configure the application to use reasonable memory. If SurrealDB doesn't expose RocksDB tuning knobs, document this as a known limitation."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "failure_mode",
      "description": "The plan moves StartLimitBurst/StartLimitInterval from [Service] to [Unit] for ngrok in Task 6 (line 258). This is correct for systemd 255 (confirmed running). However, the plan adds StartLimitBurst/StartLimitIntervalSec to the [Service] section for cohezion-surreal.service in Task 3 (implied by the service file modification). The plan doesn't specify which section to put them in for Task 3. If they go in [Service], they may be silently ignored on this systemd version, defeating the primary crash-loop prevention mechanism.",
      "recommendation": "Explicitly state in Task 3 that StartLimitBurst and StartLimitIntervalSec must go in the [Unit] section, not [Service]. Apply the same rule consistently across all tasks."
    },
    {
      "severity": "should_fix",
      "category": "completeness",
      "description": "The journal is already 1.2GB (confirmed via journalctl --disk-usage). The plan cleans up crash reports in /var/crash/ (Task 2) but doesn't address journal bloat from 129K+ service restart entries. If the journal isn't vacuumed, it will continue consuming disk and the journald stability issue that caused the Feb 17 crash may persist.",
      "recommendation": "Add a journal cleanup step to Task 2: 'sudo journalctl --vacuum-time=2d' or 'sudo journalctl --vacuum-size=256M' to reduce journal size and prevent residual journald instability."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "architecture",
      "description": "The guardian script is proposed as a single script checking multiple conditions (restart count, state duration, disk usage). If any check hangs (e.g., 'systemctl show' blocks because systemd is overloaded - which is exactly the failure mode we're protecting against), the entire guardian hangs. A 5-minute timer with a potentially-hanging script could accumulate multiple blocked instances.",
      "recommendation": "Add a timeout to the guardian's ExecStart (e.g., 'ExecStart=/usr/bin/timeout 60 /path/to/service_guardian.sh') and set TimeoutStartSec=90 in the service unit. This ensures the guardian can't become a resource problem itself."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "completeness",
      "description": "The plan mentions ngrok-tunnel logs go to /tmp/ngrok_tunnel.log with no rotation (Task 6, line 260) but only notes 'add LogsDirectory= or note for manual cleanup'. This isn't a concrete action. /tmp files survive reboots on some systems (tmpfs vs disk-backed /tmp varies). An unbounded log file is a minor disk space risk.",
      "recommendation": "Either switch ngrok to StandardOutput=journal (let journald handle rotation) or add a logrotate config. Make this a concrete action item, not a note."
    },
    {
      "severity": "suggestion",
      "category": "assumption",
      "description": "The plan assumes the rocksdb:// protocol change (Task 3) is a simple swap from file://. The current SurrealDB 2.4.1 deprecation warning suggests this, but the data was originally created with file:// protocol. If the rocksdb:// driver initializes differently (different default options, different block cache settings), it could cause subtle behavior changes or require a data migration.",
      "recommendation": "Test the protocol change separately from the MANIFEST fix. After fixing MANIFEST, first verify SurrealDB starts with file:// (known working protocol for this data), then switch to rocksdb:// and verify again. Two separate verification points rather than changing both at once."
    }
  ],
  "overall_assessment": "needs_fixes",
  "summary": "The plan correctly identifies the root cause and proposes a reasonable three-layer defense strategy. However, it has several significant gaps: (1) The guardian cannot actually stop the system-level SurrealDB service due to privilege boundaries between user and system systemd scopes - this is the most architecturally significant gap. (2) The MemoryMax=16G limit without application-level RocksDB configuration will convert an unbounded memory allocation into an OOM crash loop. (3) The SURREAL_PASS issue is more nuanced than documented - systemd doesn't evaluate shell substitutions in EnvironmentFile, so the password is a literal string, not regenerating. (4) The MANIFEST fix should be validated immediately rather than waiting for Task 8 to discover if the database is recoverable. (5) Services should be masked during the multi-task implementation window to prevent accidental re-enabling. (6) StartLimitBurst placement section ([Unit] vs [Service]) needs to be explicitly specified for all tasks. The crash-loop is still active right now (NRestarts=2248 for SurrealDB, cohezion-lab also looping), so implementation urgency is real."
}

